images": [{"time": "0", "text": "", "id": 0, "image": "engenn11le14ob07re2img01.jpg"}, {"time": "100", "text": "Some people become famous quietly, however, they do manage to change the world. Florence Nightingale changed the world quietly. Florence was born in the city of the same name, Italy in 1820. Her father was such a modern thinker that he decided that his daughter should have good education. As soon as she was ready, he had taught her languages, literature, history, and mathematics. She loved her studies from the moment she had begun them . Until Florence had decided to become a nurse, her parents were happy with her decision. They felt that nursing wasn't a profession for rich women. They wanted her to get married. She got offers of marriage, but she was so persistent in wanting to be a nurse, that when she was 31, her father agreed to let her become one.", "id": 1, "image": "engenn11le14ob07re2img01.jpg"}, {"time": "43000", "text": "In 1853, Britain entered the Crimean War where so many British soldiers were dying in Turkey that help was needed. The fighting was difficult. Soldiers also died of cholera and malaria. Immediately Florence had decided to travel to Turkey with other nurses to help the soldiers. There were such unsanitary conditions in the military hospitals that she was shocked. Before she had cleaned and organized the hospital, it was conducive to the spread of virulent disease. The food for patients was so unappealing that she had bought fruit and vegetables with her own money. She had also bought hospital equipment. It wasn't until she had demanded that the equipment, beds, mattresses and wall be scrubbed and kept clean that the numbers of dying soldiers hadn't fallen. Suddenly the mortality rate had plummeted from 60% to 2.2%. Florence was such a devoted nurse that she was often seen at night walking the halls carrying a lamp, doing her rounds.", "id": 2, "image": "engenn11le14ob07re2img02.jpg"}, {"time": "94000", "text": "That is  how she came to be known as \u2018the lady with the Lamp'.<br/>\nAfter the war, Florence had continued to work to improve conditions in hospitals. She set up nursing schools and wrote books. She fought for women's rights. When Florence died at the age of 90, she had so much for the world that it was a different place.", "id": 3, "image": "engenn11le14ob07re2img03.jpg"}], "audiosrc": "engenn11le14ob07re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn11le14ob07re2img01.jpg"}}], "template_override": 1, "type": "audio", "id": 1, "_data": [], "title": ""}]}]}